with q as (
  select $astroC_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What are the two celestial coordinates, and which Earth coordinate does each mimic?", "options": ["Altitude and azimuth, mimicking height and compass bearing", "DECLINATION (the sky's LATITUDE \u2014 degrees north/south of the celestial equator, fixed to the stars) and RIGHT ASCENSION (the sky's LONGITUDE \u2014 measured eastward from the vernal equinox), which together give every star a precise, universal two-number address", "Latitude and longitude, exactly as on Earth with the same units", "Right ascension and altitude"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The equatorial coordinate system lays Earth's own grid onto the sky. Declination is celestial latitude: how far north (+, up to +90\u00b0 at the pole) or south (\u2212, to \u221290\u00b0) of the celestial equator a star lies, fixed to the stars. Right ascension is celestial longitude, measured eastward from a natural zero point. Give any object its declination and right ascension and any astronomer, anywhere, can find the identical spot."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Right ascension is measured in HOURS, not degrees. What is the zero point, and why hours?", "options": ["Zero is Greenwich; hours are just a British convention", "Zero is the VERNAL EQUINOX (where the Sun crosses the celestial equator heading north); it is measured in hours because the sky turns 15\u00b0 \u2014 one hour of RA \u2014 every hour of time, so a star's right ascension is also a CLOCK reading that tells you when it crosses your meridian", "Zero is Polaris; hours measure the star's brightness over a night", "Zero is the Sun's current position; hours count from local noon"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The natural, star-fixed zero is the vernal equinox \u2014 the intersection of the ecliptic and the celestial equator (the 'First Point of Aries'). Right ascension runs 0\u201324 hours eastward from it, so 24h = 360\u00b0 and 1h = 15\u00b0. The choice of hours is the deep point: because the celestial sphere sweeps past at exactly one hour of RA per hour of time, a star's right ascension IS the local sidereal time at which it transits (crosses the meridian, standing highest). Celestial position and time are one system \u2014 the sky is a clock."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is finding LATITUDE at sea easy, while finding LONGITUDE was for centuries agonisingly hard?", "options": ["Latitude needs no instruments; longitude needs a telescope", "Latitude comes straight from the sky \u2014 the pole star's altitude EQUALS your latitude (one angle) \u2014 but longitude has NO natural sky reference (the spinning Earth looks the same from every meridian), so it is fundamentally a problem of TIME: you must compare local time to a reference meridian's time, and keeping accurate reference time at sea was the hard part", "Latitude is measured by the Moon, longitude by the Sun", "They are equally easy; the difficulty was purely political"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The sky hands you latitude for one careful angle: the celestial pole's altitude equals your latitude (or use the noon Sun's altitude with its known declination). Longitude has no 'Polaris' because the Earth is symmetric under rotation \u2014 every meridian looks identical to the stars. But the Earth turns 15\u00b0 per hour, so longitude is a time comparison: local noon (Sun highest) versus the time at a reference meridian at that instant, \u00d715\u00b0 per hour. The catch was knowing Greenwich time in mid-ocean, which no clock could keep until Harrison's chronometer."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "A navigator observes local noon (the Sun highest, so the local clock reads 12:00) and at that instant the chronometer, keeping Greenwich time, reads 15:00. Where are they?", "options": ["45\u00b0 east of Greenwich", "45\u00b0 WEST of Greenwich \u2014 the 3-hour difference \u00d7 15\u00b0 per hour = 45\u00b0, and because their noon happens LATER than Greenwich's (Greenwich is already at 15:00), they are to the west", "3\u00b0 west of Greenwich", "It cannot be found without also knowing the latitude"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Longitude = time difference \u00d7 15\u00b0 per hour. The gap is 3 hours (12:00 vs 15:00), giving 45\u00b0. The direction: their local noon comes 3 hours AFTER Greenwich noon (Greenwich has already reached 15:00), so the Sun reaches them later \u2014 they are west of Greenwich. Answer: 45\u00b0 W. This is the entire method of finding longitude, and its difficulty was simply carrying accurate Greenwich time across the voyage."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How did the two winning solutions to the longitude problem work, and what lasting mark did the effort leave?", "options": ["Both used telescopes; the mark was better star charts", "The MARINE CHRONOMETER (Harrison) carried accurate Greenwich time in a box, to compare with local time; the LUNAR-DISTANCE method used the sky itself as a clock, reading Greenwich time from the Moon's ~0.5\u00b0/hour drift among the stars via pre-computed tables. The effort made GREENWICH the world's prime meridian \u2014 the root of time zones and coordinated global time", "Both relied on magnetic compasses; the mark was the metric system", "Both used the Sun alone; the mark was the leap year"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Harrison's chronometer put the reference clock on the ship \u2014 set to Greenwich time in port and kept running accurately, it was compared to local solar time to yield longitude. The lunar-distance method needed no machine: the Moon moves ~0.5\u00b0 per hour against the stars, so its measured position, checked against detailed tables, gives Greenwich time directly. Because British chronometers and tables dominated navigation, Greenwich became the Prime Meridian (1884) \u2014 which is why time zones and GMT, the world's shared clock, descend from the effort to find longitude at sea."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Build the equatorial coordinate system from the ground up: define declination and right ascension, explain the vernal-equinox zero point, and explain fully why right ascension is measured in HOURS rather than degrees \u2014 and what that reveals about the relationship between celestial position and time.", "rubric": "THE PROBLEM: to specify a point on the sky EXACTLY (so any astronomer anywhere finds the identical spot) you need ADDRESSES \u2014 a coordinate grid, built by projecting Earth's own latitude/longitude onto the celestial sphere (the EQUATORIAL coordinate system). DECLINATION (celestial LATITUDE): measured from the CELESTIAL EQUATOR (the projection of Earth's equator), it gives a star's angular distance NORTH (+, up to +90\u00b0 at the north celestial pole, near Polaris) or SOUTH (\u2212, to \u221290\u00b0 at the south pole) of the equator; 0\u00b0 on the equator; measured in degrees/arcmin/arcsec. It is FIXED TO THE STARS \u2014 a star's declination does not change as the sky wheels overhead (it is a permanent property of position). RIGHT ASCENSION (celestial LONGITUDE): measured EASTWARD along the celestial equator from a zero point. THE ZERO POINT: unlike Earth's arbitrary Greenwich, the sky offers a NATURAL, star-fixed reference \u2014 the VERNAL EQUINOX, the exact point where the Sun (on the ecliptic) crosses the celestial equator heading NORTH; it is the intersection of two great circles (ecliptic and celestial equator), a precise point fixed among the stars (the 'First Point of Aries'), serving as the celestial prime meridian / zero RA. WHY HOURS (the key insight): right ascension runs 0\u201324 HOURS around the equator, so 24h = 360\u00b0 and 1h = 15\u00b0. The reason is that the sky TURNS once a day \u2014 360\u00b0 in 24 hours = 15\u00b0 per hour \u2014 so the celestial sphere sweeps past us at exactly ONE HOUR OF RA PER HOUR OF TIME. Therefore a star's right ascension is also a CLOCK READING: it tells you WHEN the star will cross your meridian (transit, standing highest/due south) \u2014 specifically, it transits when the local SIDEREAL time equals its RA. WHAT THIS REVEALS: celestial POSITION and TIME are the SAME SYSTEM \u2014 'where is that star?' and 'what time is it?' are, in a precise sense, the same question; the sky is a clock, and the coordinate grid and the astronomer's timekeeping are intimately connected. TOGETHER: RA + dec give every object a precise, universal, (nearly) permanent address (e.g. Betelgeuse ~RA 5h55m, dec +7\u00b0), turning the vague dome of scattered lights into an exactly-mapped catalogue \u2014 the foundation of positional astronomy and the first great step from SEEING the sky to MEASURING it (an exact science that predicts, to the second and arcsecond, where any object will be). A strong answer stresses the hours\u2194time equivalence as the seed of everything (and foreshadows that the coordinate grid's own zero point, the equinox, will later be found to drift)."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain how the sky lets a navigator find their position on a featureless ocean \u2014 why latitude is easy and longitude hard, how longitude reduces to a problem of time, the two solutions (chronometer and lunar distances), and how this 'closes a two-way link' and left a lasting mark on the world.", "rubric": "THE TWO-WAY LINK SET UP: the coordinate grid was built by PROJECTING Earth's latitude/longitude ONTO the sky; celestial navigation is the REVERSE \u2014 the sky hands Earth's coordinates BACK to us. LATITUDE IS EASY: the sky gives it directly \u2014 the altitude of the celestial POLE above your horizon EQUALS your latitude (measure Polaris's height with a sextant), or measure the NOON SUN's altitude and combine with the Sun's known declination for the date (latitude = 90\u00b0 \u2212 noon altitude \u00b1 declination). One careful angle \u2192 your north\u2013south position. (Historically: 'sail to the right latitude, then run east/west along it.') LONGITUDE IS HARD \u2014 AND IS A PROBLEM OF TIME: longitude has NO natural sky reference, because the Earth is SYMMETRIC under its own rotation \u2014 every meridian looks identical to the stars as it spins past (no 'Polaris of longitude'). But the Earth turns 15\u00b0 PER HOUR (360\u00b0/24h), so every HOUR of time difference = 15\u00b0 of longitude (1\u00b0 = 4 minutes of time). Local time is easy (noon = Sun highest); so if you compare your LOCAL time to the time at a REFERENCE meridian (Greenwich) AT THE SAME INSTANT, the difference \u00d7 15\u00b0/hour = your longitude (and the direction: if your noon is LATER than Greenwich's, you are WEST). THE CATCH: you must know GREENWICH time while mid-ocean \u2014 and in the 1700s no clock could keep accurate time at sea (a rocking ship wrecks a pendulum). This was THE technological problem of the age; Britain offered the LONGITUDE PRIZE (1714). TWO SOLUTIONS: (1) the MARINE CHRONOMETER \u2014 John HARRISON's clocks (H1\u2013H4) kept accurate time at sea; H4 (1761) lost only ~5 seconds to Jamaica \u2014 i.e. CARRY GREENWICH TIME IN A BOX, compare to local solar time. (2) LUNAR DISTANCES ('lunars') \u2014 use the SKY ITSELF as the clock: the Moon drifts ~0.5\u00b0/hour against the background stars, so measuring its angle from a known star and consulting pre-computed lunar TABLES reads off Greenwich time with NO mechanical clock \u2014 leaning directly on the coordinate grid and exact tables. THE LINK CLOSED + LASTING MARK: latitude from an ANGLE, longitude from a TIME comparison \u2014 the coordinates and clock of this chapter, projected out to the sky, hand the Earth's own position back. Solving longitude RESHAPED WORLD HISTORY (reliable ocean navigation, exploration, trade, empire), and because British chronometers/tables dominated, GREENWICH became the world's PRIME MERIDIAN (1884) \u2014 the root of TIME ZONES, GMT, and coordinated global time. Every time zone on a phone descends from the effort to find longitude at sea. A strong answer ties it to the chapter's core: 'the sky is a clock' (15\u00b0/hour, RA in hours) is exactly what makes the position fix possible."}]$astroC_quiz$::jsonb as questions
